# Loading a language by id

A package turns a language on by naming its id in `loom.json` — `typescript`, `python`, `go`. Loom itself is the one language built into the server. Every other language ships as a `@athrio/loom-service-<id>` package the project installs. This module turns each activated id into the `LanguageService` that serves it: it finds the package the project installed, imports it, and confirms the package exports a service for that id.

A language that fails to load costs only itself. Its sections stay at syntax highlighting — the floor every section keeps, with or without a service — while every other language serves on. Activating a language whose package is missing degrades the editor; it never breaks it.

## Importing one language's package

The loader finds a package in a workspace's store, imports it, and reads the package's default export. The loader takes `root`, the workspace whose looms the service serves, and `storeDir` finds that workspace's store. Every workspace keeps a store of its own, so two workspaces may hold one language at two versions, and each workspace loads its own. The import is dynamic, by name and at runtime, because a package is a unit the consumer installs after the editor ships, never one a build could fold in.

The file to import is the package's entry, and the package's manifest names it. The loader reads the manifest itself rather than asking Node's resolver, because the resolver inside Loom's standalone executable cannot find a package by its bare name, though it finds a file at a known path. A manifest's `exports` field names the entry in one of three shapes: as a plain path, as the path under the key `"."`, or as a set of conditions under that key, where the loader takes the `import` path, else the `default` one. `ExportsSchema` admits those three shapes. A manifest with no usable `exports` names its entry in `main`, and a manifest with neither leaves the entry at `index.js`, as Node does. `serviceEntry` reads the entry in that order.

=>

const ConditionsSchema = Schema.Struct({
  import: Schema.optional(Schema.String),
  default: Schema.optional(Schema.String),
})

const RootExportSchema = Schema.Union([Schema.String, ConditionsSchema])

const ExportsSchema = Schema.Union([Schema.String, Schema.Struct({ '.': RootExportSchema })])

const rootEntry = (root: typeof RootExportSchema.Type): Option.Option<string> =>
  typeof root === 'string'
    ? Option.some(root)
    : Option.fromNullishOr(root.import ?? root.default)

const exportedEntry = (exported: unknown): Option.Option<string> =>
  pipe(
    Schema.decodeUnknownOption(ExportsSchema)(exported),
    Option.map((decoded) => (typeof decoded === 'string' ? decoded : decoded['.'])),
    Option.flatMap(rootEntry),
  )

const serviceEntry = (manifest: ServiceManifest): string =>
  pipe(
    exportedEntry(manifest.exports),
    Option.orElse(() => Option.fromNullishOr(manifest.main)),
    Option.getOrElse(() => 'index.js'),
  )

~

A running host keeps every module it imports, under the address it imported the module from. A service that `loom add` replaces sits at the same path as the one it replaced, so an import by path alone would hand back the old module. `serviceAddress` adds the version the store holds to the entry's `file:` address, which makes each version of a service a module of its own. A language server that reloads a workspace's services after `loom add` then meets the version the store now holds. Node keys its modules by the whole address. Bun keys them by the path alone, so a server running on Bun keeps the version it imported first until it restarts.

=>

const serviceAddress = (entry: string, version: Option.Option<string>): string =>
  Option.match(version, {
    onNone: () => pathToFileURL(entry).href,
    onSome: (installed) => `${pathToFileURL(entry).href}?version=${installed}`,
  })

~

A service the store holds at another version than this Loom's was installed by another release, and it may not work inside this host: a service built against an older Effect fails on its first request. The loader still loads it, since an old service may work, but first logs a warning that names both versions and the `loom update` that brings the service in step. The warning lands before the import, so it is there even when the old service then fails.

What the import returns is unknown until checked. `isLanguageService` confirms the default export has the shape the contract requires, and the loader confirms the service's declared `id` is the one activated. A package that answers to another id, or exports something that is not a service at all, is not the language the project asked for.

A language can fail to load in three ways, and the loader logs a warning for each and yields nothing. The store may lack the package, and the warning names the `loom update` that installs it. The package may export no service for the language. Or the import itself may fail, when the entry is missing or the module throws as it loads, and the warning then carries the cause.

=>

const warnIfStale = (id: string, root: string): Effect.Effect<void> =>
  Option.match(
    Option.filter(installedVersion(id, root), (version) => version !== serviceVersion),
    {
      onNone: () => Effect.void,
      onSome: (version) =>
        Effect.logWarning(
          `${servicePackage(id)} ${version} is installed, but this Loom is ${serviceVersion} — run \`loom update ${id}\` to update it`,
        ),
    },
  )

const exportedService = (
  id: string,
  exported: unknown,
): Effect.Effect<Option.Option<LanguageService>> =>
  isLanguageService(exported) && exported.id === id
    ? Effect.succeed(Option.some(exported))
    : Effect.logWarning(`${servicePackage(id)} does not export a language service for "${id}"`).pipe(
        Effect.as(Option.none<LanguageService>()),
      )

const importService = (id: string, root: string): Effect.Effect<Option.Option<LanguageService>> =>
  Option.match(serviceManifest(id, root), {
    onNone: () =>
      Effect.logWarning(
        `language "${id}" is activated but ${servicePackage(id)} is not installed — run \`loom update ${id}\``,
      ).pipe(Effect.as(Option.none<LanguageService>())),
    onSome: (manifest) =>
      Effect.tryPromise(async () => {
        const entry = join(serviceDir(id, root), serviceEntry(manifest))
        const imported = (await import(
          serviceAddress(entry, Option.fromNullishOr(manifest.version))
        )) as { readonly default?: unknown }
        return imported.default
      }).pipe(
        Effect.flatMap((exported) => exportedService(id, exported)),
        Effect.catchCause((cause) =>
          Effect.logWarning(
            `${servicePackage(id)} could not be loaded — ${Cause.pretty(cause)}`,
          ).pipe(Effect.as(Option.none<LanguageService>())),
        ),
      ),
  })

const loadService = (id: string, root: string): Effect.Effect<Option.Option<LanguageService>> =>
  Effect.andThen(warnIfStale(id, root), importService(id, root))

## The active set

`loadActive` produces the services a project has turned on. Loom and Prose lead the set — every `.loom` has a frame and prose, so both services are always present — and each activated id follows, loaded by `loadService`. The loader drops an id equal to a built-in's, so naming `loom` or `prose` in the config never doubles it, and drops a repeated id, so activating a language twice loads it once. An id whose package does not load contributes nothing, and the set carries the languages that did.

=>

export const loadActive = (
  ids: ReadonlyArray<string>,
  root: string,
): Effect.Effect<ReadonlyArray<LanguageService>> =>
  pipe(
    ids,
    Array.filter((id) => id !== LoomLanguage.id && id !== ProseLanguage.id),
    Array.dedupe,
    (rest) => Effect.forEach(rest, (id) => loadService(id, root)),
    Effect.map((loaded) => [LoomLanguage, ProseLanguage, ...Array.getSomes(loaded)]),
  )

## What this module draws on

The loader works in Effect: `Effect` runs the load, `Option` carries a language that may not load or a version the store may not hold, `Schema` reads the shapes of a manifest's `exports`, `Cause` prints why an import failed, and `Array` with `pipe` build the set. Node's `join` forms the path of a package's entry, and `pathToFileURL` turns that path into the address the dynamic import takes. From `./LoomStore` come `servicePackage`, which names a language's package, `serviceDir`, the folder the store keeps it in, `serviceManifest`, which reads the package's manifest, `installedVersion`, which reads the version the store holds, and `serviceVersion`, the version this Loom expects. From the contract module come the `LanguageService` type and the `isLanguageService` guard; `LoomLanguage` and `ProseLanguage` are the built-in services that always lead the set.

=>

import { Array, Cause, Effect, Option, Schema, pipe } from 'effect'
import { join } from 'node:path'
import { pathToFileURL } from 'node:url'
import {
  installedVersion,
  serviceDir,
  type ServiceManifest,
  serviceManifest,
  servicePackage,
  serviceVersion,
} from './LoomStore'
import { isLanguageService, type LanguageService } from './LanguageService'
import { LoomLanguage } from './LoomLanguage'
import { ProseLanguage } from './ProseLanguage'

